\section*{Major problems 5 — The chromatic splitting conjecture, which is considerably more complicated to state.}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 318, 2026-09-25): At primes at least five, the K(3)-local sphere is claimed to retain a nonzero degree-minus-three class under rationalized K(2)-localization, obstructing unmarked strong-splitting wedge equivalences; Manuscript 2 (family 318, 2026-09-27): The canonical weak-splitting unit is claimed to have no retraction at height p for p at least five and height p+1 for p at least seven, witnessed by \(\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}\). Manuscript 3 (family 318, 2026-09-25): At prime three and height three, the overlap is claimed to lie outside the ordinary thick subcategory generated by lower local spheres, even allowing arbitrary finite cofibers and retracts of derived-complete sphere modules; Manuscript 4 (family 318, 2026-09-25): For n at least one and p greater than n+1, lower-height localization of the K(n)-local sphere is claimed to have an ordered \(2^n\)-stage filtration with the classical chromatic-splitting cofibers and canonical initial unit. Manuscript 5 (family 318, 2026-09-27): At height three and every prime at least five, the overlap is claimed to have an explicit eight-stage sphere-layer filtration with identified signed attachments; its first height-one attachment is nonzero.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For a prime $p\ge5$, let $S=S_p^\wedge$ be the completed sphere, $L_j$ localization at Johnson--Wilson $E(j)$, $L_{K(j)}$ localization at Morava $K(j)$, and $L_0$ rationalization. OpenAI claims that the canonical map
\[
L_0L_{K(3)}S\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S
\]
is nonzero on $\pi_{-3}$. Consequently it claims that $L_2L_{K(3)}S$ is not equivalent, even without compatibility with the unit summand, to
\[
L_2(S\vee\Sigma^{-1}S)\vee L_1(\Sigma^{-3}S\vee\Sigma^{-4}S)
\vee L_0(\Sigma^{-5}S\vee\Sigma^{-6}S\vee\Sigma^{-8}S\vee\Sigma^{-9}S).
\]
This is an obstruction to the proposed strong height-three wedge formula at every prime in the stated range.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 2.} Let $p\ge5$, $S=S_p^\wedge$, $L_j=L_{E(j)}$ and $L_{K(j)}$ be chromatic localizations. Put
\[
d_p=2p(p-1)-2,\qquad k_p=(p-1)^2,\qquad z_p=\beta_1^{k_p}\in\pi_{d_pk_p}S,
\]
where $\beta_1$ is the first classical beta element. OpenAI claims that the image of $z_p$ in $\pi_{d_pk_p}L_{p-1}S$ is nonzero but is killed by the unit-induced map
\[
i_{p,S}:L_{p-1}S\longrightarrow L_{p-1}L_{K(p)}S.
\]
Hence this map has no homotopy retraction. The finite input is the sphere and the height equals the prime; this does not assert such failure at every height.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 3.} At prime $3$, with completed sphere $S=S_3^\wedge$, OpenAI claims
\[
L_2L_{K(3)}S\notin\operatorname{Thick}\{L_0S,L_1S,L_2S\}
\]
in the category of $E(2)$-local $S$-modules. Here $L_j$ is $E(j)$-localization, $L_{K(3)}$ is Morava-$K(3)$ localization, and the thick subcategory allows finite cofibers, suspensions and retracts. Thus arbitrary finite attachments, including Moore corrections, cannot assemble this chromatic overlap from the indicated lower-height localized spheres.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 4.} For $n\ge1$ and prime $p>n+1$, put $S=S_p^\wedge$, $D=L_{K(n)}S$ and $X_{n,p}=L_{n-1}D$, where $L_j=L_{E(j)}$. OpenAI claims a filtration in $E(n-1)$-local $S$-modules with $2^n$ cofibers indexed by subsets $I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$:
\[
C_\varnothing=L_{n-1}S,\qquad C_I=\Sigma^{-d(I)}L_{n-\max I}S\ (I\ne\varnothing),\qquad d(I)=\sum_{i\in I}(2i-1).
\]
The empty subset is first, the remaining maxima are nondecreasing, and the first-layer map is the canonical unit. It claims a single family $y_i\in\pi_{1-2i}D$ whose ordered products $y_I$ give, for every $1\le t<n$, equivalences
\[
L_{K(t)}\left(\bigvee_{I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n-t\}}\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\xrightarrow{(y_I)}D\right).
\]
A filtered extension is asserted, not a split wedge decomposition before localization.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 5.} For every prime $p\ge5$, put $S=S_p^\wedge$ and $X=L_2L_{K(3)}S$. OpenAI claims an eight-layer filtration $0=F_0\to\cdots\to F_8\simeq X$ of $E(2)$-local $S$-modules whose ordered cofibers are
\[
L_2S,\ \Sigma^{-1}L_2S,\ \Sigma^{-3}L_1S,\ \Sigma^{-4}L_1S,\
\Sigma^{-5}H\mathbb Q_p,\ \Sigma^{-6}H\mathbb Q_p,\ \Sigma^{-8}H\mathbb Q_p,\ \Sigma^{-9}H\mathbb Q_p.
\]
Here $L_j=L_{E(j)}$, $L_{K(3)}$ is Morava-$K$ localization and $H\mathbb Q_p$ is the Eilenberg--Mac Lane spectrum. The inclusion $F_1\simeq L_2S\to X$ is the unit localization map. The displayed layers do not assert that the attaching maps vanish.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 318, 2026-09-25): Marked connected deformations produce a genuine K(2)-local detector receiving the height-three sphere, forcing factorization through the localization unit. Division-algebra and linear trace classes are compared on a determinant-lattice modification space, retaining integral cohomology before rationalization. Their nonzero rank-two restriction conflicts with the vanishing natural map that the proposed wedge would have in degree minus three. Manuscript 2 (family 318, 2026-09-27): The named beta power survives under the height-(p-1) unit by an ordinary homotopy-fixed-point detection calculation. At each upper height, a separated multiplicative filtration puts beta\_1 in degree at least two and vanishes beyond a dimension-controlled bound, killing that same power. Completion and naturality identify a nonzero lower-localized class in the unit's kernel, obstructing a retraction.

Manuscript 3 (family 318, 2026-09-25): Integral connected and etale markings retain an exceptional norm-character line on the height-two stratum. Full principal-unit action separates that line from every periodicity power carried by the permitted inputs. Comparisons through ordinary finite-stage coefficients and actual homotopy preserve the character across the whole deformation, giving a representation obstruction to any finite assembly. Manuscript 4 (family 318, 2026-09-25): Finite marking and transfer calculations first establish coefficient finiteness. A separately constructed integral family of sphere-cochain classes has normalized exterior products acting as bases under lower-height tests. Removing blocks in decreasing chromatic height yields the ordered cofibers; independent rational dimension counts supply the final block while retaining the extensions between stages.

Manuscript 5 (family 318, 2026-09-27): Integral frame comparisons construct actual bases at heights two and one, while a trace-class calculation fixes their nonzero rational degree-three comparison. A chosen compatibility homotopy feeds two fracture squares, giving the filtration and the signs of its connecting maps. The separate canonical-map obstruction then detects a nontrivial first middle attachment for these same choices.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 318, 2026-09-25): Corollary 1.2 excludes even an underlying-spectrum height-three strong wedge equivalence at every prime at least five, so one of these instances refutes the universal strong chromatic-splitting prediction.

Manuscript 2 (family 318, 2026-09-27): A nonzero sphere-kernel class in the canonical weak splitting map excludes a homotopy retraction at height p for every p>=5; the strong conjecture requires that unit summand, so these counterexamples refute its universal assertion.

Manuscript 3 (family 318, 2026-09-25): The p=n=3 counterexample rules out finite assembly by lower spheres in the specified derived-complete-sphere module category, a narrower setting than the problem’s underlying p-local sphere spectrum and unspecified maps.

Manuscript 4 (family 318, 2026-09-25): The ordered filtration has the classical local-sphere cofibers in the stated generic-prime range, but a filtration with potentially nonzero attachments is not the wedge equivalence and canonical split unit required by strong chromatic splitting.

Manuscript 5 (family 318, 2026-09-27): The ordered filtration has the classical local-sphere cofibers in the stated generic-prime range, but a filtration with potentially nonzero attachments is not the wedge equivalence and canonical split unit required by strong chromatic splitting.

